// EXPECT-FAIL: parse
// NEGATIVE test: `throw` was REMOVED from Nova (specs §5.5, plan P2-12).
//
// `throw`/`try`/`catch` stay RESERVED words so existing code gets a real explanation
// rather than "undefined identifier 'throw'", which is what deleting the keyword from
// the lexer would produce. Same treatment as `match`.
//
// Why they went — all measured, not argued:
//  1. THE VALUE NEVER SURVIVED. `nova_throw` took a `long long`; the catch bound
//     `zext(setjmp_res)` — an i32. The stdlib's own only user, web/di.nova, did
//     `throw "DI Error: Service not registered: " + key`, and web/recovery.nova caught
//     it and logged:  [RECOVERY] Caught exception: 8472
//     — the string pointer, truncated to 32 bits and re-stringified as a number. The
//     message was destroyed at the throw. web/mediator.nova had already given up on
//     `err` and passed a hardcoded string to its exception handlers.
//  2. NO UNWINDING ⇒ GUARANTEED LEAKS. ARC released only the catching frame's try-block
//     locals; every frame between the throw and the catch leaked everything it owned.
//     (F5 §3.4e found `break`/`continue` released nothing — "the exits that JUMP".
//     A throw was a third such exit, covered by no rule at all.)
//  3. UB WHERE IT WAS ACTUALLY USED. setjmp/longjmp out of a C++20 coroutine is
//     undefined behaviour, and the one stdlib consumer was a pipeline behavior running
//     inside an async HTTP handler.
//
// The replacement is `T | Error` (plan P2-13): errors are values, checked by the type
// system, message intact, nothing to unwind.
@test
fn t(): void {
    throw "this must not compile";
}
